member.summary:
v69: the player pressed the voice level key and asks for  (0-based in IServerApi.VoiceLevels;
 is the one they speak at). Return false to refuse - they stay where they are, and the mode
tells them why if it wants to. IServerApi.SetPlayerVoiceLevel does not come here.

v65: the player's game answered an order that carried this kind (,
): it applied it ( true) or gave up (false - a game that cannot do the
kind, a build it does not know, an order that never could be applied). The record kept what the mode set either way.

v83: a player's edit in the map editor is about to be applied (their scope allowed it). Return false to refuse it - tell them why;
the editor puts the objects back as they were. A plot builder's limits go here: where they build (the places), what (the meshes).
The mode's own functions (CreateMapObject ...) and the admins' /map never come here.

v62: the third-person view the server gave the player (IServerApi.SetPlayerThirdPerson; 
unless a mode changed it). Which view the player looks from while it is  is their own
choice, which the server does not see.

v51: the look the player's body wears over its soul's own (IServerApi.SetPlayerLook, a LookRequest the mode took);
 = the soul's own. It lasts the session, through respawns; a mode that keeps it saves it
(Look.ToString) and gives it back at the next visit.

v53: what the horse's saddlebags hold - the server's record: what SetHorseItems made them, changed by what the player whose game
holds them (the owner, else the rider, else the one who simulates the horse) moved in or out and the server accepted (v62);
null = not a horse. A copy that shows more than this has the excess taken out under [audit] mode = "enforce".

v76: the game mode's own identity of one item (a letter, a signed contract): its id, and the name and text the game's item tooltip
shows for it. A tagged entry is one item (Amount 1). GetPlayerInventory lists the player's tagged items one by one with theirs;
SetPlayerInventory keeps a tagged item the player holds by its id and makes a missing one with its tag.

v86: the item class whose model the item shows (its name, id, English name or GUID - the server keeps the GUID): in the hands, on the
belt and back, lying on the ground and on the other players' screens. The item stays what it is (its stats, its weight and price);
only its look is the other's. null = its own.

v41: a gate of the level (a portcullis, a drawbridge) as the server holds it. Kind = "portcullis" / "drawbridge" (v59: "gate"
for one whose ends are layers); Cycle = "closed", "opening", "open", "closing" (open = passable: a portcullis raised, a
drawbridge lowered); Openness = 0 closed .. 1 open, walking across while a cycle runs; X Y Z where it stands (metres);
OpenSeconds / CloseSeconds = how long a full cycle takes that way (0 = the piece has no clip that way and a cycle is refused;
both 0 for a "gate", which is at the other end at once).

v41: a gate of the level (a portcullis, a drawbridge) as the server holds it. Kind = "portcullis" / "drawbridge" (v59: "gate"
for one whose ends are layers); Cycle = "closed", "opening", "open", "closing" (open = passable: a portcullis raised, a
drawbridge lowered); Openness = 0 closed .. 1 open, walking across while a cycle runs; X Y Z where it stands (metres);
OpenSeconds / CloseSeconds = how long a full cycle takes that way (0 = the piece has no clip that way and a cycle is refused;
both 0 for a "gate", which is at the other end at once).

v41: the answer to a gate cycle request. Accepted = a cycle started; Reason = why not, worded after the gate's name ("is
already open", "is already closing", "has no opening animation", "is not a gate this server knows"; "" when accepted);
Opening = the direction taken or refused; Seconds = how long the motion still has to run (0 when nothing moved).

v41: the answer to a gate cycle request. Accepted = a cycle started; Reason = why not, worded after the gate's name ("is
already open", "is already closing", "has no opening animation", "is not a gate this server knows"; "" when accepted);
Opening = the direction taken or refused; Seconds = how long the motion still has to run (0 when nothing moved).

v69: one voice level a player can switch to with the voice level key () - its name
on the player's HUD and how far a voice carries at it, in metres (0 = the server's default range,
).

